[{:db/id 17592186198448 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bffc4e85-37a7-58e6-9707-94d9ac87c744"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198978 :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186197738 :answer-field/correct 17592186197742 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-bffc4e85-37a7-58e6-9707-94d9ac87c744" :question/id #uuid "bffc4e85-37a7-58e6-9707-94d9ac87c744" :question/math-academy-id "q-177893" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-45554d58-8407-5972-941e-77d5ad8113b4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-45554d58-8407-5972-941e-77d5ad8113b4" :answer-field/id #uuid "45554d58-8407-5972-941e-77d5ad8113b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e705ebf2-c558-54c0-816f-602c5af2facd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1bd4d334-2269-5bfe-a24c-a58aafbef588" "answer-a8c1505c-5ad9-511e-a22d-30a3286bfbdb" "answer-9164dcc8-fbe9-5d34-a2f1-c8be97a45615" "answer-4a46d5a3-3c48-5050-85ff-c52a5b9baaee" "answer-e705ebf2-c558-54c0-816f-602c5af2facd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bd4d334-2269-5bfe-a24c-a58aafbef588" :answer/id #uuid "1bd4d334-2269-5bfe-a24c-a58aafbef588" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8c1505c-5ad9-511e-a22d-30a3286bfbdb" :answer/id #uuid "a8c1505c-5ad9-511e-a22d-30a3286bfbdb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9164dcc8-fbe9-5d34-a2f1-c8be97a45615" :answer/id #uuid "9164dcc8-fbe9-5d34-a2f1-c8be97a45615" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a46d5a3-3c48-5050-85ff-c52a5b9baaee" :answer/id #uuid "4a46d5a3-3c48-5050-85ff-c52a5b9baaee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e705ebf2-c558-54c0-816f-602c5af2facd" :answer/id #uuid "e705ebf2-c558-54c0-816f-602c5af2facd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ac6fbe63-89e3-55f0-b383-00653c46559a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ac6fbe63-89e3-55f0-b383-00653c46559a" :question/id #uuid "ac6fbe63-89e3-55f0-b383-00653c46559a" :question/math-academy-id "q-178025" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n\nII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthogonal\n\nIII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthonormal" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, both $Q({u}_{1})$ and $Q({u}_{2})$ are unit vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q({u}_{1})‖ & =‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}+(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}}=1 \\\\ ‖Q({u}_{2})‖ & =‖{u}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}+(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5243b6a3-407c-5377-9662-ad587ee4e1e3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5243b6a3-407c-5377-9662-ad587ee4e1e3" :answer-field/id #uuid "5243b6a3-407c-5377-9662-ad587ee4e1e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e4112fbf-91d8-5bb1-acad-1777ebe798bc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-85bffa49-25ed-5277-8eb0-2bf0d83a3acf" "answer-eb713c79-d5f4-5d03-8da7-db12deacf3d2" "answer-e4112fbf-91d8-5bb1-acad-1777ebe798bc" "answer-f04b535e-e446-5693-b598-2a2ae8017998" "answer-acfdb839-bbb9-5d75-a684-8e0ba2f1180c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85bffa49-25ed-5277-8eb0-2bf0d83a3acf" :answer/id #uuid "85bffa49-25ed-5277-8eb0-2bf0d83a3acf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb713c79-d5f4-5d03-8da7-db12deacf3d2" :answer/id #uuid "eb713c79-d5f4-5d03-8da7-db12deacf3d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4112fbf-91d8-5bb1-acad-1777ebe798bc" :answer/id #uuid "e4112fbf-91d8-5bb1-acad-1777ebe798bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f04b535e-e446-5693-b598-2a2ae8017998" :answer/id #uuid "f04b535e-e446-5693-b598-2a2ae8017998" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acfdb839-bbb9-5d75-a684-8e0ba2f1180c" :answer/id #uuid "acfdb839-bbb9-5d75-a684-8e0ba2f1180c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186284764 :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n\nII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthogonal\n\nIII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthonormal" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-33e49b6c-0f59-53bc-8c69-4b46de238ff6" :activity/id #uuid "33e49b6c-0f59-53bc-8c69-4b46de238ff6" :activity/title "Orthogonal Linear Transformations" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050910 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-198647b9-7961-5263-912d-818d3e048db4" "step-3d1ac785-35a7-523f-9e93-3c458c7662c9" "step-87144feb-48ca-5cf7-a2fa-4efd26384df9" "step-024575a8-c8f0-5c99-9c1d-bf6941663ece" "step-04e86d6f-be2b-5bc9-ac7f-9c117105b2fe"] :activity/first-step "step-198647b9-7961-5263-912d-818d3e048db4" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-198647b9-7961-5263-912d-818d3e048db4" :step/id #uuid "198647b9-7961-5263-912d-818d3e048db4" :step/content 17592186198978 :step/next "step-3d1ac785-35a7-523f-9e93-3c458c7662c9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3d1ac785-35a7-523f-9e93-3c458c7662c9" :step/id #uuid "3d1ac785-35a7-523f-9e93-3c458c7662c9" :step/content 17592186406654 :step/next "step-87144feb-48ca-5cf7-a2fa-4efd26384df9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-87144feb-48ca-5cf7-a2fa-4efd26384df9" :step/id #uuid "87144feb-48ca-5cf7-a2fa-4efd26384df9" :step/content "question-bffc4e85-37a7-58e6-9707-94d9ac87c744" :step/next "step-024575a8-c8f0-5c99-9c1d-bf6941663ece" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-024575a8-c8f0-5c99-9c1d-bf6941663ece" :step/id #uuid "024575a8-c8f0-5c99-9c1d-bf6941663ece" :step/content "question-ac6fbe63-89e3-55f0-b383-00653c46559a" :step/next "step-04e86d6f-be2b-5bc9-ac7f-9c117105b2fe" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-04e86d6f-be2b-5bc9-ac7f-9c117105b2fe" :step/id #uuid "04e86d6f-be2b-5bc9-ac7f-9c11